    "source_contracts": [
      {
        "id": "supplies",
        "detail": "DS specifies VDD 2.7 to 3.6 V and VDDA=VDD, with a typical external 2.2 uF VCAP capacitor. C542RCT6 suffix 6 temperature limits and electrical conditions remain those of DS Table 17. These values do not qualify the board power arrangement.",
        "evidence": [
          "DS15135:p10",
          "DS15135:p51",
          "RM0522:p225",
          "RM0522:p226"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "PVD",
        "detail": "Threshold is fixed by hardware despite the programmable detector name. PVDO is valid only with PVDE enabled. PVD uses EXTI16 and IRQ1; peripheral enable alone does not establish an EXTI/NVIC route. DS Table 19 values are characterization values, not production tested unless stated. SBS PVDL can lock detector configuration. EXTI16 to IRQ1 is derived from RM Table62 and Table100, not a new independently owned EXTI resource. DS falling threshold2.89-2.96 V lies inside the operating supply range; PVDO=1 alone does not establish an out-of-spec supply fault.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p228",
          "RM0522:p243",
          "RM0522:p245",
          "RM0522:p246",
          "DS15135:p11",
          "DS15135:p52",
          "header.IRQ",
          "HAL.PVD",
          "RM0522:p441"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "entry_and_wake",
        "detail": "WFI, WFE and sleep-on-exit have distinct pending-interrupt/event behavior. Pending requests can prevent entry. Stop must coordinate peripheral flags, EXTI pending state and enabled wake paths; masking only NVIC while a peripheral interrupt remains enabled can retain clocks in Stop 0. No universal clear-all sequence is authorized. ES2.6.1 (A for Z and Y) describes an LPTIM interrupt condition preventing Stop entry and prescribes RCC reset rather than clearing LPTIM ENABLE. Apply the established SDK lifecycle restrictions; a generic sleep setter does not implement that workaround.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p233",
          "RM0522:p234",
          "RM0522:p236",
          "RM0522:p237",
          "RM0522:p238",
          "ES0676:p6",
          "ES0676:p2",
          "DS15135:p6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "stop_retention",
        "detail": "Stop retention is selectable for the exact SRAM partitions. Live stacks, DMA buffers, code/data and other owners must be outside any powered-down partition. Standby loses SRAM and resumes via a reset/boot path; Stop resumes execution but does not automatically restore the prior system clock tree.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p229",
          "RM0522:p230",
          "RM0522:p234",
          "RM0522:p235",
          "RM0522:p236",
          "RM0522:p240"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "peripheral_clocks",
        "detail": "RM requires suitable peripheral enable, low-power enable and independent clock choices for autonomous Stop operation. Flash or APB activity can delay entry; enabled ADC/DAC may consume power. Family Table 55 is not permission to use absent C542 peripherals, and per-peripheral Stop capability remains to be checked.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p231",
          "RM0522:p232",
          "RM0522:p236",
          "RM0522:p237"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "wake_pin_arming",
        "detail": "Wake pulls must agree with GPIO pull configuration or use no pull. Enabling a wake pin at its active level generates an additional wake event. Flags clear through CWUF, NRST or wake-enable clearing as documented. Configuration, enabling and flag consumption require shared GPIO/PWR ownership and an application event-loss policy. PA0 also carries TAMP_IN2/ADC1_IN0/COMP1_INP and PA2 TAMP_IN3/ADC1_IN2/COMP2_INM/OPAMP1_VINM0; PC13 carries TAMP_IN1/RTC_TS/RTC_OUT1. Shared pad ownership and analog/alternate configuration must be reconciled before wake use.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p246",
          "RM0522:p247",
          "RM0522:p248",
          "DS15135:p39",
          "DS15135:p40",
          "LL.wake_enable"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "IO_retention",
        "detail": "IORETEN captures output state into pull configuration for Standby, with separate JTAG retention and exceptions for standby functions. The cited JTAG group is PA13, PA14, PA15 and PB4. Retained pulls persist after wake until released, and can oppose newly configured GPIO pulls. DBG_STANDBY prevents IO retention. PC13 RTC and PC14/15 LSE functions require separate ownership. Wake/tamper/analog use on PA0 and PA2 and wake/tamper/RTC use on PC13 must be coordinated with retained GPIO pulls.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p239",
          "RM0522:p240",
          "RM0522:p241",
          "RM0522:p248",
          "DS15135:p39",
          "DS15135:p40"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "status_outputs",
        "detail": "PC2 AF0 CSLEEP and PC3 AF0 CSTOP encode run 00, sleep 10 and Stop 11 in the RM signal order. They are not driven in Standby. Package route correspondence establishes no board load or measurement qualification.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p242",
          "DS15135:p47"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "privilege",
        "detail": "PRIV=1 restricts PWR registers to privileged access with unprivileged read-as-zero/write-ignored behavior. PRIVCFGR itself always requires privileged writes while remaining readable. Successful SDK return without readback is not proof that a protected write took effect.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p242",
          "RM0522:p249",
          "HAL.IO_privilege",
          "LL.clear_privilege"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "RTC_protection",
        "detail": "RTCCR.DRTCP bit 0 is 0 for protection and 1 for access. ES2.7.3 prints a blank field name, preserved as such; binding it to DRTCP is a derived cross-source identification, not a corrected literal quotation. RTC_SCR, TAMP_SCR and TAMP_CR2.BKERASE writes while protected can hang the system. PWR access, RTC WPR, privilege and required RTC/TAMP sequencing are separate gates; toggling DRTCP alone is not a complete protocol. RM241 explicitly preserves PWR_RTCCR and IORETR across Standby wake; do not assume DRTCP protected merely because execution restarted at boot. Readback and boot-cause policy remain needed. ES2.7.3 status is N for both Z and Y.",
        "evidence": [
          "RM0522:p226",
          "RM0522:p245",
          "ES0676:p8",
          "header.fields",
          "LL.RTC",
          "HAL.RTC",
          "RM0522:p241",
          "ES0676:p2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "PC13_LSE",
        "detail": "Exact LQFP64 WKUP4 uses PC13. ES2.2.2 says input or output toggling of PC13 can disturb LSE crystal operation on LQFP; external bypass is not affected. ES2.2.1 forbids analog bypass and ES2.2.3 forbids low drive. These cross-peripheral restrictions survive low-power configuration and cannot be enforced by independent GPIO/PWR/RCC setters alone.",
        "evidence": [
          "ES0676:p4",
          "ES0676:p5",
          "DS15135:p39"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "SDK_gates": [
      {
        "id": "wake_config_shift",
        "detail": "HAL SetConfigWakeupPin recomputes position and 1UL << position after removing the final selected bit. STM32_POSITION_VAL(0) reaches CLZ(0)=32 in the cited CMSIS GCC implementation. With 32-bit unsigned long, the final shift by 32 is C undefined behavior even for a valid nonempty mask. No compiler output or hardware failure has been observed; this source path is not operationally admitted.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.wake_config",
          "family.position",
          "CMSIS.position"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "wake_config_validation_and_race",
        "detail": "Only the NULL check is optional runtime validation; mask, polarity and pull are debug assertions. WUCR is read before these checks, modified as a whole snapshot and written once. Invalid values can enter unmasked shifts; concurrent changes can be overwritten. The function preserves wake enables and does not coordinate active level, GPIO pulls, pending flags or privilege readback.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.maps_guards",
          "HAL.wake_config",
          "HAL.wake_access",
          "LL.pull"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "entry_global_state",
        "detail": "Sleep changes SLEEPDEEP; Stop sets it then clears it after return, rather than restoring the prior bit. Stop writes LPMS and does not restore clocks or LPMS. Standby sets SLEEPDEEP/LPMS and executes WFI without post-return cleanup if entry was prevented. These void calls provide no successful-entry receipt and no complete wake-source setup. Table60 requires chosen WUF and RTC/TAMP flags cleared for entry, but HAL Standby does not qualify them. If entry is prevented, later WFI/WFE or sleep-on-exit may use the retained deep-sleep/Standby selection when other conditions permit, without another explicit Standby setter.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.entry",
          "LL.mode",
          "RM0522:p233",
          "RM0522:p241"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "core_event_consumption",
        "detail": "ClearCorePendingEvent performs SEV then WFE. It consumes the core event register; it is not a peripheral, EXTI or NVIC pending clear. SLEEPONEXIT and SEVONPEND setters affect shared core state.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.entry",
          "HAL.core_previous"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "previous_mode_observation",
        "detail": "Previous-mode query gives SBF precedence, otherwise interprets STOPF using current LPMS. Retained/stale flags or a later LPMS change can make this unsuitable as a chronological transition receipt. Both LL clear-status helpers set CSSF, which clears SBF and STOPF together.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.core_previous",
          "LL.clear_status",
          "RM0522:p244"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "retention_argument_guards",
        "detail": "Memory map indexing relies partly on debug assertions and optional runtime guards. The whole-memory Enable function uses USE_HAL_CHECK_PARAM==1 while the whole-memory Disable function uses only defined(USE_HAL_CHECK_PARAM), so a value of 0 produces asymmetric checks. Page arithmetic lacks explicit nonzero count and complete overflow exclusion. For SRAM2, page_idx=2/page_nbr=0 passes the shown guards, executes no loop and returns HAL_OK without changing retention. Bound bank, page start and positive count before entry, with checked arithmetic. With debug assertions disabled, SRAM2 page_idx=2,page_nbr=0xFFFFFFFF wraps (page_idx-1)+page_nbr to0, passes runtime range checks and enters an extremely long loop; status becomes HAL_ERROR on out-of-range iterations, but there is no early break. This is source arithmetic, not executed behavior.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.maps_guards",
          "HAL.retention",
          "HAL.pages_enable",
          "HAL.pages_disable_query"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "retention_queries",
        "detail": "HAL whole-memory getter reports disabled for paginated SRAM2; it is not a whole-bank retention proof. LL combined-mask query tests whether all masked shutdown bits are set, so a partially retained combined selection can report enabled. Use per-page evidence and exclude zero or invalid masks.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.retention",
          "HAL.pages_disable_query",
          "LL.retention"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "privilege_attribute",
        "detail": "HAL SetPrivAttr checks privileged execution at runtime and item validity only with a debug assertion, but does not assert or runtime-check priv_attr enumeration. LL uses item*priv_attr without masking the result; out-of-domain values can spill outside the intended PRIV bit. Valid input and exclusive register ownership remain caller gates.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.IO_privilege",
          "LL.clear_privilege",
          "HAL.enums"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "PVD_and_RTC_access",
        "detail": "PVD getter reads raw status without qualifying PVDE; enable/disable does not verify SBS lock or privilege acceptance. RTC protection helpers only change DRTCP and do not themselves execute the complete erratum-safe RTC/TAMP sequence.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.PVD",
          "HAL.RTC",
          "LL.RTC",
          "RM0522:p246",
          "ES0676:p8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "wake_getter_and_IO_validation",
        "detail": "GetConfigWakeupPin has only debug guards for pointer and single-pin domain; a zero mask yields position32 and invalid C shifts in polarity extraction. Invalid masks can also select unsupported fields. EnableIORetention ORs the unmasked caller value into an IORETR read-modify-write, so valid enumeration and concurrent ownership are caller requirements. No generated behavior is assumed for invalid C shifts.",
        "evidence": [
          "HAL.wake_access",
          "HAL.IO_privilege",
          "family.position",
          "CMSIS.position"
        ]
      }
    ],
